1790556902435007	agentbus-8lbb0p	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3v2	{"description":"Establish, with tools that can fail, the properties agentbus wait and the transcript tailer rest on, and fix the defects found while surveying.\n\nRouting (rust-formal-verification skill): no property here fails per-step decomposition, so no Verus; no shared-memory concurrency worth loom (hook.rs Relaxed fetch_add and ui.rs stop flag are correct); no peer protocol for Stateright. The fit is proptest for runtime-bounded state (completion::Index, tail::MultiTail::poll) and Kani for the fixed-width timestamp parser. Crash consistency across processes and the filesystem is deterministic-simulation territory, tracked separately and punted.\n\nDone when: the two confirmed bugs are fixed with regression properties, the model-based and chunking properties pass, the Kani harness passes and was shown to fail under mutation, and a cargo-mutants rerun shows the surviving mutants in completion.rs/tail.rs/wait.rs are closed or justified.\n\nTooling on this machine: kani, cargo-fuzz and cargo-mutants are installed; proptest is not yet a dependency.","kind":"goal","labels":["verification"],"title":"Verify the wait/tail correctness core"}	blake3:P7Ak9cebyxsYaeurNBFHF-0D7Y3TiVGPBNrtC9KnVzk
1790556939224679	agentbus-8lbb0p	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-38j	{"description":"Run cargo mutants -f src/completion.rs -f src/tail.rs -f src/wait.rs before writing new properties, and record the surviving mutants in this bone. They are the measured gap the property tests should close, and the baseline the final rerun is compared against. Note: cargo-mutants drives cargo test only; it says nothing about the Kani harness.","kind":"task","labels":["verification"],"parent":"bn-3v2","size":"xs","title":"Baseline surviving mutants in completion, tail and wait"}	blake3:O6KAMWeZh9W0A1a-RRiF39IJ85UHUNMO4lCR1jang5U
1790556940687800	agentbus-8lbb0p	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2i9	{"description":"Add proptest under [dev-dependencies]. Keep it test-only: the shipped binary's dependency set (crossterm, ratatui, serde_json, signal-hook) should not grow.","kind":"task","labels":["verification"],"parent":"bn-3v2","size":"xs","title":"Add proptest as a dev-dependency"}	blake3:X_jDJTeK-c_nHuz9YZruP9baU8LBLtPt11uPLLJkNng
1790556941929692	agentbus-8lbb0p	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1q3	{"description":"tail::MultiTail::poll (src/tail.rs) runs String::from_utf8_lossy on each read chunk and stores partial as String. A multibyte character split across two polls, or across a 64 KiB read inside one poll, becomes U+FFFD pairs.\n\nReproduced on a scratch copy: file contains b\"x\" + first byte of \"é\"; poll; append second byte + newline; poll returns [\"x\\u{FFFD}\\u{FFFD}\"] instead of [\"xé\"]. Large result_full payloads make the 64 KiB case likely, because serde_json does not escape non-ASCII.\n\nFix: keep Cursor.partial as Vec<u8>, split on b'\\n', decode each complete line once. TailCheckpoint.partial and the cursors.json encoding (src/cursor.rs, VERSION 1, stored as a JSON string) must carry bytes: bump the cursor VERSION or add a byte-safe field, and keep loading version-1 files. Checkpoint the partial bytes as a string only when they are valid UTF-8.\n\nRegression property (proptest): for arbitrary byte content with arbitrary non-ASCII, arbitrary append chunking, and arbitrary poll / checkpoint / restore points, the emitted lines equal the non-empty lines of the full content (with \\r\\n trimmed), each exactly once. Mutation check: reintroduce per-chunk lossy decoding and confirm the property fails.","kind":"bug","labels":["verification"],"parent":"bn-3v2","size":"s","title":"Tailer corrupts multibyte UTF-8 split across a read boundary"}	blake3:QGyi5IPhfIvC750KGX2ovH2VCqHXmniRT2qqsl_SmTE
1790556943118464	agentbus-8lbb0p	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-3s2	{"description":"completion::Index::newest_since (src/completion.rs) decides Expired by comparing since with the first retained record's timestamp. Records are ordered by generation, not time, so a pruned record newer than since can sit behind a retained record that is older. The result is Pending, and wait then blocks and returns the next turn's answer instead of reporting expired.\n\nReproduced on a scratch copy: limits max_records=1; push completions a@12:00:50 then b@12:00:10; newest_since(\"a\", 12:00:30) returns Pending, expected Expired.\n\nSeverity is low (it needs --since near the retention edge, after 10k completions or 7 days) but it breaks the contract wait documents.\n\nFix: track the maximum epoch ever pruned (monotone, like floor; persist it in completions.json with a load default that keeps old files readable), and return Expired when since <= that watermark and no retained match exists. Add the reproduction as a regression test.","kind":"bug","labels":["verification"],"parent":"bn-3v2","size":"s","title":"wait --since reports Pending when the answer was pruned"}	blake3:6TAfZRGze36aDWCXzFYkyZu1coKbz_vzf_SeVxDVGdI
1790556944055559	agentbus-8lbb0p	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-wo6	{"description":"The property agentbus wait rests on: first_after(s, w) returns exactly the first record for s with generation > w that an unbounded reference log holds, or Expired. It never returns Pending when such a record was pruned, and never a different record. newest_since(s, since) holds the same contract by timestamp.\n\nAlso assert the structural invariant after every step: retained generations strictly increase and lie within [floor, generation]; generation and floor never decrease. Each operation preserves these on its own (the per-step decomposition), so a sampled state machine is the right tool; no deductive verifier is needed.\n\nOperations to generate: push_events with mixed sessions and out-of-order or missing timestamps (missing timestamps exercise retained_at), prune under small max_records / max_bytes / max_age limits, and encode -> load round-trips. Index::load resets limits to default, so the harness must reapply the test limits after load or make that explicit.\n\nMutation checks: drop the floor update in remove_oldest, flip > to >= in first_after, and remove the Expired branch; each must fail the property. Any new defect gets its own bug bone under this goal.","kind":"task","labels":["verification"],"parent":"bn-3v2","size":"m","title":"Model-based property for completion::Index against an unbounded reference"}	blake3:79Z9XtvtGOnNgYdf8AX7jTI0tcTQpZTuBXBaK0eF1VI
1790556945153849	agentbus-8lbb0p	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-n6g	{"description":"event::iso_to_epoch (src/event.rs) parses a fixed 19-byte prefix, so Kani covers every input completely. It feeds durations, completion age pruning and wait --since.\n\nHarnesses: (1) no panic for any 19..=24 byte input; (2) Some implies well-formed fields: 4-digit year, month 1-12, day valid for the month, hour < 24, minute and second < 60, no sign characters; (3) for two well-formed timestamps, lexicographic order of the 19-byte prefix equals numeric order of the result.\n\nExpect (2) to fail as written: the function currently accepts month 13, hour 99 and a '+026' year. Tighten validation to make it pass, and keep the existing tests green.\n\nPut the harnesses behind #[cfg(kani)]. If str::parse makes CBMC slow, parse the digits by hand in the function itself; the harness must call the real function, not a copy. Mutation check: remove a range check and confirm Kani fails; add assert!(false) once to confirm the assumptions are satisfiable. Add a just recipe for cargo kani.","kind":"task","labels":["verification"],"parent":"bn-3v2","size":"s","title":"Kani harness for iso_to_epoch"}	blake3:QExgd67Ytxx2BqfLTpyDemwdfeY9MYjUVITXZ3PSzIE
1790556946295737	agentbus-8lbb0p	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-ru0	{"description":"The most important correctness surface outside this goal's tools: hook spool -> register/inbox journals (dedup by _spool_id) -> inbox compaction, and main.rs publishing completions, event log, snapshot and cursors.json in that order.\n\nQuestions to settle: after a crash between the completion write and the cursor checkpoint, does a restart republish a turn_end under a new generation? That looks at-least-once by design; if so, document it as intended. Can inbox compaction drop a _spool_id while a stale .ready file survives, causing a re-append?\n\nThese involve processes and a filesystem under crashes, so they need the rust-deterministic-simulation skill (fault-injected FS, crash points, seeded replay), not proptest or Kani. Punted: scope it before starting.","kind":"task","labels":["verification"],"parent":"bn-3v2","size":"l","title":"Deterministic simulation of the spool, journal and cursor crash windows","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:xNKREIPvVCnKc1oyKWBKJsxuHvEVxs6wYCeIEgq43fU
1790556947564538	agentbus-8lbb0p	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-33u	{"description":"Rerun cargo mutants on completion.rs, tail.rs and wait.rs and compare with the baseline bone. Close each remaining surviving mutant or justify it. List in the goal what is proven (Kani), what is sampled (proptest), and what is untested (crash windows, deferred to the simulation bone).","kind":"task","labels":["verification"],"parent":"bn-3v2","size":"xs","title":"Rerun mutants and record the verified surface"}	blake3:JeP3P1_-_Z1UOmrv_ridNw87JeWkhBxh3vLC4cGL3hw
